From: Harry Shapiro <habs@panix.com>
Subject: FWD/FYI: Panix upgrades announcement

a conscious being, The Panix Staff wrote:
>From upgrades Tue Nov 16 12:10:34 1993
Date: Tue, 16 Nov 1993 12:10:34 -0500
Message-Id: <199311161710.AA16254@panix.com>
From: The Panix Staff <staff>
Subject: Copy of the Panix upgrades announcement
Apparently-To: habs@panix.com

Here is a copy of the announcement posting in the panix.announce newsgroup.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

In the next few weeks, Panix will be undergoing a number of major changes.
In fact, we will change more in the last six weeks of 1993 than in all the
previous weeks of this year. This note explains what those changes will be,
and how they will affect you. Please read it carefully.

The changes are, in the order discussed below:
1) New Panix staff
2) New Panix computer, disk, etc.
3) New network wiring
4) New Long Island numbers
5) New phone lines in NYC
6) SLIP and PPP service
7) Clarinet news
8) New backup software



1) As Panix has grown, the load on Jim, me, and the volunteer staff has
become almost unmanageable. To deal with this, we've hired our first
full-time employee: Mara Chibnik. Mara was Panix's first user, back in
1989, so I guess it's only appropriate that she be our first employee.
We're very pleased and proud to have her with us. (That's some truly awful
CorpSpeak, I guess, but it's also true.)

We are, BTW, still looking for at least one more employee. Please let us
know if you know anyone who you think is qualified.

Mara will be starting with us this Wednesday, answering staff email and
questions posted in the Panix newsgroups. Please, don't send her questions
to her regular email address- the correct address, as always, will be
"staff".

2) Panix is adding a second user machine, since our first is already
fully-loaded and we're adding many new lines soon. This machine will be
fully equal to the one we already have, a Sparc 10/40 with 80MB of memory.
We also have another 2.5GB "Barracuda" disk drive for it.

All this hardware will be installed Tuesday or Wednesday night, although it
will be a few days before we start putting new users on it.

There will be one obvious change resulting from this: Some of you will soon
have to type "panix2" when dialing in, instead of "panix", to get to your
account. Similarly, you would have to telnet to "panix2.panix.com" to get
to it from the internet. HOWEVER, YOUR EMAIL ADDRESSES WILL NOT CHANGE. If
your email address is now "xyz@panix.com", it will remain "xyz.panix.com"
regardless of which machine you wind up using. (It is likely that there may
be one more change in host names, perhaps half a year from now, and that
that will be the last one; regardless, your email address will NEVER
change, so you will never have to worry about notifying people about your
host-name change.)

Those of you being moved to the new machine will be notified well in
advance, and mistakes will be handled gracefully (in other words, if you
log into the wrong machine, it will tell you what you are doing wrong,
instead of simply denying access).

Finger requests, like email addresses, will not have to change. (Those of
you who remember the days of "sun.panix.com" should note that this is a
major improvement. :-)

"Hey", "yo", "reply", "what", and "huh" have all been upgraded; they will
work correctly across all Panix machines, with no change in the command
syntax.  Many thanks to Don Samek (des) for improving his already excellent
software!

There may be one side effect of this change. Currently, the "phone" command
only works between users on a single machine. We are working on replacement
software which will allow phone to function bewteen machines. We hope to
have a replacement in place by the time we divide users up among the two
machines, but if we cannot, you'll have to fall back on email for the time
being. I recognize that this may cause some difficulty for a few of you;
for that, I apologize, and if you contact us by email we might be able to
make special arrangements. However, I don't expect this problem to last
more than a few days (if it comes up at all).

3) We're converting our network from a "thin ethernet" to "10BaseT". This
will have no visible effect other than to make our wiring much more
dependable. This change will happen gradually over the next few days; it
has already started.

4) Our Long Island lines will be up "soon". This _should_ mean today, but
we've had so many setbacks (all but one attributable to NY Telephone, of
course) that I'm not making any promises... still, if it's not today, it
will be _really_ soon. There will be 12 low-speed and 12 high-speed lines
at first, with more added as necessary.

5) Our NYC lines will be up "soon". Unlike the Long Island "soon", this may
mean "this week, if we're lucky". More likely, it mean "middle of next
week". This all depends on NY Telephone. I am sure that everyone is as
tired of hearing this excuse as I am giving it, so for your amusement, here
are a few of the latest new excuses, courtesy of NYTel's research division:
 - Oh, um, we can't find the copper wires even though we just planted 300
   new pairs last month.
 - We still can't find the copper. Seems that 80% of the plans for this CO
   are wrong.
 - The good news is, we're going to give you lines on your fiber optics. The
   bad news is, now we have to install all sorts of new equipment.
 - We've ordered your cabinet and Slick-96, it should be here in a week or
   so. [actually, it took 3 weeks]
 - You need to put a special power outlet in for our cabinet. No, we couldn't
   possibly have told you that before we tried to install it.
 - We need to order line cards for the CO. No, of course we couldn't do that
   until the cabinet was standing useless in your office.
 - We need to put up a terminal in your office. No, of course we can't do
   that _this_ week, because we're working on the NYC Marathon. [it's a
   miracle the Marathon wasn't delayed six months...]
 - Yes, we got the line cards. But now they have to be installed.

It occurs to me that NYTel might be good at one thing: David Letterman top-
ten lists...

I think you all get the picture. The only good thing I can say about this
is that I think that they're running out of excuses-- despite their best
efforts, all but two of the steps necessary to put in our lines have been
taken. The installation of the line cards should happen today or tomorrow,
and then the last step, programming the switch for our setup, should take a
couple of days. (No, don't ask me why they can't do it all simultaneously.
I think it has something to do with subhuman intelligence, but I could be
wrong. It could be a matter of sub-garden-slug intelligence.)

6) SLIP and PPP services will be available in December. We're shooting for
Dec. 15, but we are not committed to that date. We're committed to _trying_
to do it by that date. The key factors determining our success or failure
will be (you guessed it) NY Telephone's timely delivery (sure) of phone
lines. The situation here, though, is much better than it has been for our
regular lines, since by that time the fiber-optics stuff will be all
settled. We'll start out with ten lines and see how popular the service is.

We will be offering personal connections for only $35/month! As far as I
know, that is many times cheaper than the alternatives.

Please don't send us email for more details about this yet- I'll post when
it's finalized.

7) We're starting to work out the details for our Clarinet newsfeed. It
will be available around the end of December. Again, this is subject to
delays or speedups depending on how easy or difficult our lives are made by
other aspects of running Panix. (Yes, that _does_ mean NYTel, among other
things.)

8) We've upgraded our 5GB tape backup unit, by adding a compression chip,
so that we'll be able to back up the increased load of user files. It will
also be re-installed Tuesday or Wednesday night. We've also bought some new
backup software, which will allow us to back up the entire machine while
it's in use. Previously, we had to go down for full backups.


In addition to all the changes listed here, a few other things are going on.
The most significant is the installation of our "Secure server", but that
shouldn't affect anyone on Panix except admins and would-be crackers. Other
more minor changes will include the installation of one or two smaller Sparcs
as utility servers (for things like DNS, NTP, erpc, tftp, and Kerberos), and
(hopefully for the very last time) a disk re-org that will improve performance.

I hope that there won't need to be much discussio of this, but if there is,
please post in panix.upgrade.

/a


