Lefty writes: 

   Ray claims
   >
   >When Bruce was kicked off, the same people were complaining.

   This is incorrect.  I do not believe that I made any complaint when Bruce
   White was removed from the list.  If you have evidence to the contrary,
   please produce it; otherwise, I'd like to see this statement retracted.

I don't think that when a college professor says "let's not see the
same hands", he is necessarily saying that precisely the same set of
people are raising their hands this time as the time before.  In this
context, "the same" is a colloquialism.

That said, I would probably have phrased the sentence "I think a lot
of the same people were complaining."  This is an example of a
semantics concept that has been explained briefly to me at a party,
which I call "semantic sugar", but which has some fancier name from
General Semantics or some such discipline -- I would muchly appreciate
if someone would post its name and a brief, more precise description
of it.

I tend to use this kind of phrasing because I know that I don't know
anything like all of what there is to know, and I would hate to be
accused of being dishonest or malicious because I made an unqualified
statement that turned out to be false.  This is even more the case
when referring to other people's intelligence, motives, or other
internal states than when referring to physical phenomena which I may
know only by conjecture, study, or report.  As far as that goes, even
my interpretations of what I see with my own eyes may be faulty -- I
have to have working assumptions to go on in order to function, but
I'm not wed to being right about anything (except my spelling :-) ).

I think that if people (even such people as read this list) wrote with
this in mind, there might be less flamage, partly because less
dogmatic statements are less likely to trip people's defenses.

