Date: Tue, 16 Nov 93 13:14:39 -0800
From: freeman@maspar.com (Jay R. Freeman)
Subject: Dinosaurs among us

    Last summer, _Jurassic_Park_ generated some Extropian interest in dinosaur
hunting and feeding strategies.  The other day I watched a snowy egret stalk
and capture a mouse, and it was a little spooky how much its motions reminded
me of the movie sequence involving _T._rex_ and Gennaro.  I was watching
from less than 5 meters as the egret walked through the pickleweed zone of
a tidal marsh at extreme high tide.  Its body was several times as tall as
the rather even vegetation.  The hunt was cautious.  The egret moved slowly
and delicately, pausing frequently to extend its head slowly forward, perhaps
not to disturb prey.  The lunge was very fast, and it came up with a mouse
held crosswise in its bill.  During the next twenty or thirty seconds it
shook its head several times, rotating it about the axis of the bill,
manifestly to break the spine of the victim.  Then it repositioned the
animal in its bill and swallowed it whole.
    There has been increasing evidence for some time that birds are a
derived group of therapod dinosaurs -- a current vertebrate zoology text
which is very cautious about many other matters says flat out "therapod
dinosaurs include ... all birds ..." (and my book's at home so I can't
give the reference handily).  Thus I suspect that the _JP_ dinosaur people
studied exactly these habits in preparation for their special effects.
My observation indicates how well they did their homework, and makes me
suspect that they may have come close to the truth of what _T._rex_
actually did.

